ENCAPSULATION is the most fundamental pillar. It combines data and behavior into a class and controls access. The private fields cannot be accessed directly from outside — only through public getter/setter methods. This is not just about hiding data — it is about CONTROLLING how data changes. A bank account's balance should never be set to a negative value. Without encapsulation, any code could write `account.balance = -5000`. With encapsulation, `withdraw(amount)` validates the amount, checks minimum balance, and throws InsufficientFundsException if the balance would go below zero.

```java
// ═══ ENCAPSULATION: private data + controlled access ═══
public class Account {
    private BigDecimal balance;          // PRIVATE — cannot be accessed directly
    private final String accountId;      // PRIVATE + FINAL — immutable after creation

    public Account(String accountId, BigDecimal initialBalance) {
        this.accountId = accountId;
        this.balance = initialBalance;
    }

    // PUBLIC method — the ONLY way to modify balance
    public void withdraw(BigDecimal amount) {
        if (amount.signum() <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Amount must be positive");
        if (balance.subtract(amount).compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) < 0) {
            throw new InsufficientFundsException("Balance " + balance + " insufficient for " + amount);
        }
        balance = balance.subtract(amount);
        // Audit log, notification, etc. — all controlled here
    }

    public BigDecimal getBalance() { return balance; }  // READ access — no SET access!
    // No setBalance() method! Balance can ONLY change through deposit()/withdraw().
}
```

ABSTRACTION hides the implementation details behind a clean interface. The caller interacts with WHAT the system does, not HOW it does it. In Java, abstraction is achieved through abstract classes and interfaces. An interface `PaymentService` declares `transfer(from, to, amount)` — the caller doesn't know if it uses NEFT, RTGS, or UPI internally. The implementation can change without affecting callers.

```java
// ═══ ABSTRACTION: hide HOW, expose WHAT ═══
public interface PaymentService {
    TransferResult transfer(String fromAccount, String toAccount, BigDecimal amount);
    // Caller knows WHAT: "transfer money between accounts"
    // Caller doesn't know HOW: debit → validate → credit → audit → notify
}

// Implementation 1: NEFT
public class NeftPaymentService implements PaymentService {
    @Override
    public TransferResult transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        // 1. Validate accounts exist
        // 2. Check balance sufficient
        // 3. Debit source (RBI clearing)
        // 4. Credit destination (next-day settlement)
        // 5. Generate UTR number
        // 6. Send SMS notification
        // 7. Write audit log
        // Caller sees NONE of this complexity!
        return new TransferResult("UTR12345", "SUCCESS");
    }
}

// Implementation 2: UPI — same interface, completely different internals
public class UpiPaymentService implements PaymentService {
    @Override
    public TransferResult transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Completely different: VPA resolution → NPCI switch → instant credit
        // But the CALLER CODE doesn't change!
        return new TransferResult("UPI98765", "SUCCESS");
    }
}
```

INHERITANCE establishes an IS-A relationship where a child class extends a parent class, inheriting all non-private fields and methods. The child can ADD new behavior and OVERRIDE existing behavior. In banking: SavingsAccount IS-A Account, CurrentAccount IS-A Account. Both share: accountId, balance, deposit(), withdraw(). SavingsAccount ADDS: interestRate, calculateInterest(). CurrentAccount ADDS: overdraftLimit, getEffectiveBalance().

```java
// ═══ INHERITANCE: code reuse + IS-A relationship ═══
public class Account {
    protected String accountId;
    protected BigDecimal balance;
    protected String holderName;

    public void deposit(BigDecimal amount) {
        balance = balance.add(amount);
    }

    public void withdraw(BigDecimal amount) {
        if (balance.compareTo(amount) < 0) throw new InsufficientFundsException();
        balance = balance.subtract(amount);
    }
}

// SavingsAccount IS-A Account — inherits deposit(), withdraw(), balance
public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    private BigDecimal interestRate;  // ADDED behavior

    public BigDecimal calculateInterest() {  // ADDED method
        return balance.multiply(interestRate);
    }
}

// CurrentAccount IS-A Account — inherits same fields + methods
public class CurrentAccount extends Account {
    private BigDecimal overdraftLimit;  // ADDED behavior

    @Override
    public void withdraw(BigDecimal amount) {  // OVERRIDDEN behavior
        BigDecimal effective = balance.add(overdraftLimit);
        if (effective.compareTo(amount) < 0) throw new InsufficientFundsException();
        balance = balance.subtract(amount);
        // CurrentAccount allows withdrawal up to balance + overdraft!
    }
}
```

POLYMORPHISM means the same method call executes different code depending on the actual object type at runtime. The variable type is the PARENT (Account), but the actual object is the CHILD (SavingsAccount or CurrentAccount). When `account.withdraw(amount)` is called, the JVM looks at the ACTUAL object type (not the variable type) and calls the correct version of withdraw(). This is called DYNAMIC DISPATCH — the method resolution happens at runtime, not compile time.

```java
// ═══ POLYMORPHISM: same call, different behavior ═══
public class InterestBatchJob {

    public void processMonthEnd(List<Account> allAccounts) {
        for (Account account : allAccounts) {
            // POLYMORPHISM: the SAME code handles different account types!
            if (account instanceof SavingsAccount sa) {
                BigDecimal interest = sa.calculateInterest();
                account.deposit(interest);  // polymorphic deposit()
            }
            // No need to know all possible account types at compile time!
        }
    }

    // Even simpler with polymorphic method:
    public void processWithdrawal(Account account, BigDecimal amount) {
        account.withdraw(amount);
        // If account is SavingsAccount → calls Account.withdraw() (strict balance check)
        // If account is CurrentAccount → calls CurrentAccount.withdraw() (allows overdraft!)
        // SAME code, DIFFERENT behavior at runtime!
    }
}
```

HOW THE FOUR PILLARS WORK TOGETHER in a real banking system:

```java
// ENCAPSULATION: Account.balance is private — changed only through deposit/withdraw
// ABSTRACTION: PaymentService.transfer() hides NEFT/UPI/RTGS complexity
// INHERITANCE: SavingsAccount extends Account — reuses deposit/withdraw
// POLYMORPHISM: account.withdraw(amount) behaves differently for each account type

// The real power: adding a NEW account type requires ZERO changes to existing code!
public class NRIAccount extends Account {
    private String foreignCurrency;
    private BigDecimal exchangeRate;

    @Override
    public void withdraw(BigDecimal amountInINR) {
        BigDecimal amountInForeign = amountInINR.divide(exchangeRate, 2, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
        // Custom validation for NRI: check FEMA compliance
        // Check repatriation limits
        super.withdraw(amountInINR);
    }
}
// The InterestBatchJob, PaymentService, TransferController — NONE of them need changes!
// They all work with "Account" — polymorphism handles the rest.
```

