sections.s2_technical:
The four pillars (encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, polymorphism) were covered in depth in Q1. Here we focus on the THREE RELATIONSHIP CONCEPTS that complete the OOP picture.

ASSOCIATION is the most general relationship between two objects. Object A knows about Object B and can call its methods. Neither owns the other. Both have independent lifecycles. In Java, one class holds a reference to another — typically through a field, constructor parameter, or method parameter.

```java
// ═══ ASSOCIATION: TradeService USES NotificationService ═══
// Neither owns the other. Both exist independently.
// If NotificationService is replaced, TradeService still exists.
public class TradeService {
    private final NotificationService notificationService;  // ASSOCIATION: uses

    public TradeService(NotificationService notificationService) {
        this.notificationService = notificationService;
        // TradeService doesn't CREATE notificationService — it's injected (loose coupling)
    }

    public void executeTrade(Trade trade) {
        // ... execute trade logic ...
        notificationService.sendTradeConfirmation(trade);  // uses the associated service
    }
}
// TradeService and NotificationService are ASSOCIATED — they collaborate
// but neither OWNS the other. Spring injects the dependency.
```

AGGREGATION is a WEAK HAS-A relationship. The parent contains children, but children have independent lifecycles — they can exist without the parent. If the parent is destroyed, children survive. In Java: the parent holds a collection of children, but does NOT control their creation or destruction.

```java
// ═══ AGGREGATION: Department HAS Traders (weak ownership) ═══
// Traders exist independently of the department.
// If the department is dissolved, traders move to another department.
public class TradingDepartment {
    private String departmentName;
    private List<Trader> traders;  // AGGREGATION: has traders, doesn't own them

    public TradingDepartment(String name) {
        this.departmentName = name;
        this.traders = new ArrayList<>();
    }

    public void addTrader(Trader trader) {
        traders.add(trader);
        // Department does NOT create the trader — trader existed before being added
    }

    public void removeTrader(Trader trader) {
        traders.remove(trader);
        // Trader is NOT destroyed — just removed from this department
        // Trader can join another department
    }
}

// Key difference from composition:
// department = null;  → traders still exist in memory!
// In JPA: NO cascade delete. Removing department does NOT delete traders.
```

COMPOSITION is a STRONG HAS-A relationship. The parent OWNS children — children cannot exist without the parent. If the parent is destroyed, children are ALSO destroyed. The parent controls the entire lifecycle: creation, modification, and destruction of children.

```java
// ═══ COMPOSITION: Account OWNS Transactions (strong ownership) ═══
// Transactions have NO meaning without their account.
// If the account is closed (deleted), all its transactions are also deleted.
public class Account {
    private String accountId;
    private BigDecimal balance;
    private final List<Transaction> transactions = new ArrayList<>();  // COMPOSITION: owns

    public void deposit(BigDecimal amount) {
        balance = balance.add(amount);
        // Account CREATES the transaction — it owns it
        transactions.add(new Transaction("CREDIT", amount, LocalDateTime.now()));
    }

    public void withdraw(BigDecimal amount) {
        balance = balance.subtract(amount);
        transactions.add(new Transaction("DEBIT", amount, LocalDateTime.now()));
    }

    // When account is closed → all transactions deleted (lifecycle bound)
    public void close() {
        transactions.clear();  // transactions destroyed with account
        // In JPA: @OneToMany(cascade = ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
        // DELETE FROM transactions WHERE account_id = 'SAV2001'
        // DELETE FROM accounts WHERE account_id = 'SAV2001'
    }
}

// Transaction CANNOT exist without an Account:
// new Transaction() alone is meaningless — which account? what context?
```

The VISUAL DIFFERENCE in UML and code:

```java
// ASSOCIATION:  A ——→ B         (uses — line with arrow)
//   class A { B b; }            (A references B)

// AGGREGATION:  A ◇——→ B        (has — hollow diamond)
//   class A { List<B> items; }  (A has B's, B survives without A)

// COMPOSITION:  A ◆——→ B        (owns — filled diamond)
//   class A { List<B> parts; }  (A owns B's, B dies with A)
//   In JPA: cascade=ALL + orphanRemoval=true
```

Why OOP is important — three benefits:

```java
// 1. MODULARITY: each class is a self-contained unit
//    Account class: handles balance, deposits, withdrawals
//    TradeService: handles trade execution
//    NotificationService: handles SMS/email
//    Each can be developed, tested, and deployed independently.

// 2. MAINTAINABILITY: change one class without breaking others
//    Change NotificationService from SMS to email → TradeService unchanged
//    Add NRIAccount → InterestBatchService unchanged (polymorphism)
//    Fix a bug in withdraw() → deposit() is unaffected (encapsulation)

// 3. REAL-WORLD MODELING: objects map to real things
//    Account object = real bank account
//    Trade object = real stock trade
//    Customer object = real customer
//    The code structure mirrors the business domain.
```

