sections.s2_technical:
The confusion arises because both "hide" something. But WHAT they hide is different. Encapsulation hides DATA (fields). Abstraction hides IMPLEMENTATION (logic). Encapsulation restricts WHO can change data. Abstraction restricts WHAT details the caller sees.

ENCAPSULATION — WHY we need it and WHEN to use it:

Without encapsulation, any code can corrupt data:

```java
// ❌ WITHOUT ENCAPSULATION: fields are public
public class Account {
    public BigDecimal balance;       // anyone can set this!
    public String status;            // anyone can change this!
}

// ANY code anywhere can do:
account.balance = new BigDecimal("-999999");  // NEGATIVE balance — CORRUPTED!
account.status = "SUPERSECRET_ADMIN";        // invalid status — CORRUPTED!
// No validation, no audit trail, no notification.
// A single bug in ANY class can destroy data integrity.

// ✅ WITH ENCAPSULATION: controlled access
public class Account {
    private BigDecimal balance;      // PROTECTED — only methods can change
    private String status;           // PROTECTED

    public void withdraw(BigDecimal amount) {
        if (amount.signum() <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Amount must be positive");
        if (balance.compareTo(amount) < 0) throw new InsufficientFundsException();
        if (!"ACTIVE".equals(status)) throw new AccountFrozenException();
        balance = balance.subtract(amount);
        auditLog.record("DEBIT", amount);
        notificationService.send("Withdrawal of Rs." + amount);
    }
    // Balance CANNOT be negative. Status CANNOT be invalid. Every change is validated + logged.
}
```

WHEN to use encapsulation — specific scenarios:

```java
// SCENARIO 1: Data must always be valid
private BigDecimal balance;  // cannot be negative
private int age;             // cannot be < 0 or > 150
private String email;        // must match regex pattern

// SCENARIO 2: Changes must be tracked/audited
private String status;
public void changeStatus(String newStatus) {
    auditLog.record("Status changed from " + this.status + " to " + newStatus);
    this.status = newStatus;
}

// SCENARIO 3: Changes must trigger side effects
private BigDecimal balance;
public void withdraw(BigDecimal amount) {
    balance = balance.subtract(amount);
    sendSmsNotification(amount);  // side effect on every change
    updateDashboard();             // side effect on every change
}

// SCENARIO 4: Internal representation may change
private long balancePaise;  // stored as paise internally
public BigDecimal getBalance() {
    return BigDecimal.valueOf(balancePaise, 2);  // exposed as rupees
}
// Caller sees BigDecimal. Internal storage is long (paise). Can change without affecting caller.
```

ABSTRACTION — WHY we need it and WHEN to use it:

Without abstraction, callers depend on implementation details:

```java
// ❌ WITHOUT ABSTRACTION: caller knows ALL internal steps
public class PaymentController {
    public void handleTransfer(TransferRequest request) {
        // Caller must know and execute ALL 8 steps:
        Account fromAccount = accountRepo.findById(request.from());
        Account toAccount = accountRepo.findById(request.to());
        if (fromAccount.getBalance().compareTo(request.amount()) < 0)
            throw new InsufficientFundsException();
        fromAccount.setBalance(fromAccount.getBalance().subtract(request.amount()));
        toAccount.setBalance(toAccount.getBalance().add(request.amount()));
        accountRepo.save(fromAccount);
        accountRepo.save(toAccount);
        auditService.logTransfer(fromAccount, toAccount, request.amount());
        notificationService.sendSms(fromAccount.getPhone(), "Debited Rs." + request.amount());
    }
    // 10 lines of implementation details in the CONTROLLER!
    // If step 5 changes (add compliance check), controller must change.
    // If another controller also does transfers, ALL of them must change.
}

// ✅ WITH ABSTRACTION: caller sees ONE method
public class PaymentController {
    private final PaymentService paymentService;

    public void handleTransfer(TransferRequest request) {
        paymentService.transfer(request.from(), request.to(), request.amount());
        // ONE LINE. Caller doesn't know the 8 internal steps.
        // Change implementation (add compliance check) → controller UNCHANGED.
    }
}

public class PaymentService {
    public TransferResult transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        // ALL 8 steps hidden here — ABSTRACTED away from caller
        validateAccounts(from, to);
        checkBalance(from, amount);
        debitAccount(from, amount);
        creditAccount(to, amount);
        checkCompliance(from, to, amount);  // new step — caller doesn't know!
        logAudit(from, to, amount);
        sendNotification(from, amount);
        return new TransferResult("SUCCESS", generateUTR());
    }
}
```

WHEN to use abstraction — specific scenarios:

```java
// SCENARIO 1: Complex multi-step process
paymentService.transfer(from, to, amount);  // 8 steps hidden

// SCENARIO 2: Multiple implementations (strategy pattern)
interface InterestCalculator {
    BigDecimal calculate(Account account);
}
class SavingsInterestCalculator implements InterestCalculator { ... }
class FDInterestCalculator implements InterestCalculator { ... }
// Caller works with InterestCalculator — doesn't know which implementation

// SCENARIO 3: Implementation may change
interface NotificationService {
    void send(String message);
}
class SmsNotificationService implements NotificationService { ... }
class EmailNotificationService implements NotificationService { ... }
// Switch from SMS to Email — caller code unchanged

// SCENARIO 4: Third-party library wrapper
class MarketDataService {
    public BigDecimal getCurrentPrice(String symbol) {
        // Internally: calls Bloomberg API, handles authentication, retries, caching
        // Caller sees: one method call
    }
}
```

HOW THEY WORK TOGETHER:

```java
// Encapsulation PROTECTS the data inside the abstraction's implementation:
public class PaymentService {
    // ENCAPSULATION: private fields
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;
    private final AuditService auditService;

    // ABSTRACTION: simple public interface
    public TransferResult transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        // Implementation uses ENCAPSULATED account:
        Account fromAccount = accountRepo.findById(from);
        fromAccount.withdraw(amount);  // withdraw validates (encapsulation!)
        // ...
    }
}
// Abstraction hides the HOW. Encapsulation protects the DATA inside.
// Both are needed. Both are different. Both work together.
```

