sections.s2_technical:
OVERLOADING — rules and JVM resolution:

The compiler resolves overloaded methods by matching the argument types at COMPILE TIME. It picks the MOST SPECIFIC matching signature. If no exact match exists, it tries: (1) widening (int → long → float → double), (2) autoboxing (int → Integer), (3) varargs (int...).

```java
public class FeeCalculator {

    // OVERLOAD 1: calculate fee for a trade amount
    public BigDecimal calculateFee(BigDecimal amount) {
        return amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.001"));  // 0.1% brokerage
    }

    // OVERLOAD 2: calculate fee for amount + fee type
    public BigDecimal calculateFee(BigDecimal amount, String feeType) {
        return switch (feeType) {
            case "BROKERAGE" -> amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.001"));
            case "GST" -> calculateFee(amount).multiply(new BigDecimal("0.18"));
            default -> BigDecimal.ZERO;
        };
    }

    // OVERLOAD 3: calculate fee for amount + fee type + is-intraday flag
    public BigDecimal calculateFee(BigDecimal amount, String feeType, boolean isIntraday) {
        BigDecimal base = calculateFee(amount, feeType);
        return isIntraday ? base.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.5")) : base;  // 50% discount for intraday
    }
}

// COMPILE-TIME resolution:
FeeCalculator calc = new FeeCalculator();
calc.calculateFee(new BigDecimal("100000"));                        // → calls OVERLOAD 1
calc.calculateFee(new BigDecimal("100000"), "BROKERAGE");           // → calls OVERLOAD 2
calc.calculateFee(new BigDecimal("100000"), "BROKERAGE", true);     // → calls OVERLOAD 3
// The COMPILER decides which method to call based on argument types. NO runtime decision needed.
```

Overloading rules:
- MUST change: parameter list (number, type, or order)
- CAN change: return type, access modifier, exceptions
- CANNOT overload by ONLY changing return type (compile error: ambiguous)

```java
// ✅ Valid overloads:
BigDecimal calculateFee(BigDecimal amount)
BigDecimal calculateFee(BigDecimal amount, String type)
BigDecimal calculateFee(int quantity, BigDecimal price)

// ❌ INVALID: same params, different return type only
BigDecimal calculateFee(BigDecimal amount)
String calculateFee(BigDecimal amount)  // COMPILE ERROR! Same signature, different return.
// Compiler can't decide which to call: calc.calculateFee(amount) → BigDecimal or String?
```

OVERRIDING — rules and JVM resolution:

The JVM resolves overridden methods at RUNTIME using dynamic dispatch. It looks at the ACTUAL object type in the heap, not the variable type in the code. This is why `Account account = new SavingsAccount(...)` calls SavingsAccount's methods — because the actual object IS a SavingsAccount.

```java
public class Account {
    public BigDecimal calculateInterest() {
        return BigDecimal.ZERO;  // base: no interest
    }

    public String describe() {
        return "Generic Account";
    }
}

public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    private BigDecimal rate = new BigDecimal("0.04");

    @Override  // OVERRIDE: same name + same params
    public BigDecimal calculateInterest() {
        return getBalance().multiply(rate);  // DIFFERENT behavior!
    }

    @Override
    public String describe() {
        return "Savings Account (4% annual)";
    }
}

public class CurrentAccount extends Account {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal calculateInterest() {
        return BigDecimal.ZERO;  // current: explicitly zero
    }

    @Override
    public String describe() {
        return "Current Account (no interest)";
    }
}

// RUNTIME resolution — the JVM decides:
Account account1 = new SavingsAccount(...);  // variable type: Account. Actual: SavingsAccount
Account account2 = new CurrentAccount(...);  // variable type: Account. Actual: CurrentAccount

account1.calculateInterest();  // JVM: actual type = SavingsAccount → calls SavingsAccount.calculateInterest()
account2.calculateInterest();  // JVM: actual type = CurrentAccount → calls CurrentAccount.calculateInterest()
// The COMPILER doesn't know which version will run. The JVM decides AT RUNTIME.
```

Overriding rules:
- MUST keep: same method name + same parameter list + same or covariant return type
- MUST keep: same or WIDER access modifier (protected → public OK, public → private NOT OK)
- MUST keep: same or NARROWER checked exceptions
- CANNOT override: static methods (hidden, not overridden), final methods, private methods
- SHOULD use: `@Override` annotation (compile-time check)

```java
// COVARIANT return type — overriding CAN narrow the return type:
class Account { Account createCopy() { return new Account(); } }
class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    @Override
    SavingsAccount createCopy() { return new SavingsAccount(); }  // ✅ Covariant: SavingsAccount IS-A Account
}

// ACCESS: can WIDEN, not narrow:
class Account { protected void process() {} }
class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    @Override public void process() {}    // ✅ protected → public (wider) OK
}
class CurrentAccount extends Account {
    // @Override private void process() {}  // ❌ protected → private (narrower) COMPILE ERROR!
}
```

HOW THE JVM RESOLVES — the bytecode difference:

```java
// OVERLOADING: compiler generates invokevirtual with the SPECIFIC signature
// Bytecode: invokevirtual FeeCalculator.calculateFee(Ljava/math/BigDecimal;)  ← signature baked at compile time

// OVERRIDING: compiler generates invokevirtual with the PARENT signature
// Bytecode: invokevirtual Account.calculateInterest()V
// AT RUNTIME: JVM looks at the actual object's vtable (virtual method table)
// vtable for SavingsAccount: calculateInterest → SavingsAccount.calculateInterest
// vtable for CurrentAccount: calculateInterest → CurrentAccount.calculateInterest
// JVM dispatches to the CORRECT version based on the actual object type
```

