sections.s2_technical:
The key insight: an abstract class is "allowed to have unfinished business." It can declare that it implements an interface without actually providing the method body. The unimplemented methods remain abstract — passed down to the next subclass.

```java
// ═══ LAYER 1: Base abstract class with state ═══
public abstract class Account {
    protected String accountId;
    protected BigDecimal balance;

    protected Account(String id, BigDecimal bal) {
        this.accountId = id;
        this.balance = bal;
    }

    public void deposit(BigDecimal amount) { balance = balance.add(amount); }
    public abstract String getAccountType();  // abstract — subclass decides
}

// ═══ LAYER 2: Abstract class EXTENDS Account AND IMPLEMENTS 2 interfaces ═══
public abstract class InterestBearingAccount extends Account implements Auditable, Taxable {
    // Extends Account: inherits accountId, balance, deposit()
    // Implements Auditable: MUST provide generateAuditReport() — or stay abstract
    // Implements Taxable: MUST provide calculateTax() — or stay abstract

    protected BigDecimal annualRate;

    protected InterestBearingAccount(String id, BigDecimal bal, BigDecimal rate) {
        super(id, bal);  // calls Account constructor
        this.annualRate = rate;
    }

    // Provides ONE abstract method from Account:
    // getAccountType() — still abstract! Passed to concrete subclass.

    // Provides a CONCRETE method:
    public BigDecimal calculateMonthlyInterest() {
        return balance.multiply(annualRate).divide(BigDecimal.valueOf(12), 2, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
    }

    // Does NOT implement Auditable.generateAuditReport() → remains abstract
    // Does NOT implement Taxable.calculateTax() → remains abstract
    // This class MUST be abstract because it has unimplemented methods!
}

// ═══ LAYER 3: Concrete class — MUST implement ALL remaining abstract methods ═══
public class SavingsAccount extends InterestBearingAccount {

    public SavingsAccount(String id, BigDecimal bal, BigDecimal rate) {
        super(id, bal, rate);
    }

    // From Account (via InterestBearingAccount): getAccountType()
    @Override
    public String getAccountType() { return "SAVINGS"; }

    // From Auditable (via InterestBearingAccount): generateAuditReport()
    @Override
    public List<String> generateAuditReport() {
        return List.of("Account: " + accountId, "Balance: Rs." + balance);
    }

    // From Taxable (via InterestBearingAccount): calculateTax()
    @Override
    public BigDecimal calculateTax() {
        BigDecimal interest = calculateMonthlyInterest().multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(12));
        return interest.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.30"));  // 30% tax on interest
    }
}
// SavingsAccount is CONCRETE — ALL abstract methods implemented.
// Chain: Account → InterestBearingAccount → SavingsAccount
// Interfaces: Auditable + Taxable resolved at SavingsAccount level.
```

What happens at each layer:

```java
// LAYER 1 (Account):
//   abstract methods: getAccountType()
//   concrete methods: deposit()

// LAYER 2 (InterestBearingAccount extends Account implements Auditable, Taxable):
//   inherited abstract: getAccountType() — NOT implemented (still abstract)
//   new concrete: calculateMonthlyInterest()
//   from Auditable: generateAuditReport() — NOT implemented (still abstract)
//   from Taxable: calculateTax() — NOT implemented (still abstract)
//   Total abstract methods: 3 → class MUST be abstract

// LAYER 3 (SavingsAccount extends InterestBearingAccount):
//   implements: getAccountType() ✅
//   implements: generateAuditReport() ✅
//   implements: calculateTax() ✅
//   Total abstract methods: 0 → class CAN be concrete ✅
```

An abstract class can also PARTIALLY implement interface methods:

```java
public abstract class InterestBearingAccount extends Account implements Auditable, Taxable {

    // Implements Auditable.generateAuditReport() HERE — concrete subclass doesn't need to!
    @Override
    public List<String> generateAuditReport() {
        return List.of("Account: " + accountId, "Type: " + getAccountType());
    }

    // Leaves Taxable.calculateTax() abstract — concrete subclass MUST implement
    // @Override public abstract BigDecimal calculateTax();  ← implicit from interface
}
// Now SavingsAccount only needs to implement: getAccountType() + calculateTax()
// generateAuditReport() is already done by InterestBearingAccount!
```

