USE 1 — Access fields (resolve shadowing):

```java
public class Account {
    protected String name = "Account";
}

public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    private String name = "SavingsAccount";  // SHADOWS parent's name field!

    public void printNames() {
        System.out.println(this.name);   // "SavingsAccount" — THIS class's field
        System.out.println(super.name);  // "Account" — PARENT class's field
        // Without this/super: 'name' refers to the closest scope = this.name
    }
}
// Field shadowing is rare and discouraged. But this/super resolves it if it occurs.
```

USE 2 — Call methods (invoke parent's overridden version):

```java
public class Account {
    public void withdraw(BigDecimal amount) {
        if (balance.compareTo(amount) < 0) throw new InsufficientFundsException();
        balance = balance.subtract(amount);
        log("DEBIT Rs." + amount);
    }
}

public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    @Override
    public void withdraw(BigDecimal amount) {
        // EXTRA validation: minimum balance check
        if (balance.subtract(amount).compareTo(MIN_BALANCE) < 0) {
            throw new MinimumBalanceException("Would go below Rs." + MIN_BALANCE);
        }
        super.withdraw(amount);  // CALL PARENT'S withdraw — does the actual debit + logging
        // Without super.withdraw(): we'd have to duplicate the debit + logging logic!
        // super.withdraw() = "do what Account.withdraw() does"
    }
}

// this.withdraw() vs super.withdraw():
public class CurrentAccount extends Account {
    @Override
    public void withdraw(BigDecimal amount) {
        // this.withdraw(amount) → INFINITE RECURSION! Calls THIS method again!
        // super.withdraw(amount) → calls Account.withdraw() — correct!
        super.withdraw(amount);
    }
}
```

USE 3 — Call constructors (chaining):

```java
public class Account {
    private final String accountId;
    private BigDecimal balance;
    private final String holderName;

    // Primary constructor:
    public Account(String accountId, String holderName, BigDecimal balance) {
        this.accountId = accountId;
        this.holderName = holderName;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    // Convenience constructor — chains to primary using this():
    public Account(String accountId, String holderName) {
        this(accountId, holderName, BigDecimal.ZERO);
        // this() = call another constructor in THIS class
        // MUST be the FIRST statement!
    }
}

public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    private final BigDecimal interestRate;

    // Calls PARENT constructor using super():
    public SavingsAccount(String accountId, String holderName, BigDecimal balance, BigDecimal rate) {
        super(accountId, holderName, balance);
        // super() = call Account's constructor
        // MUST be the FIRST statement!
        this.interestRate = rate;
    }

    // Convenience constructor — chains to THIS class constructor:
    public SavingsAccount(String accountId, String holderName) {
        this(accountId, holderName, BigDecimal.ZERO, new BigDecimal("0.04"));
        // this() = call SavingsAccount(String, String, BigDecimal, BigDecimal) above
        // MUST be first statement!
    }

    // ❌ CANNOT use both this() and super() in same constructor:
    // public SavingsAccount(String id) {
    //     super(id, "Unknown", BigDecimal.ZERO);  // first statement
    //     this(id, "Unknown");  // ❌ COMPILE ERROR! Second constructor call not allowed!
    // }
}
```

Implicit super() — if you don't write any constructor call:

```java
public class CurrentAccount extends Account {
    public CurrentAccount() {
        // Java inserts: super(); → calls Account's no-arg constructor
        // If Account doesn't HAVE a no-arg constructor → COMPILE ERROR!
    }
}
// Rule: if parent has NO no-arg constructor, child MUST explicitly call super(args).
```

`this` as a reference — passing the current object:

```java
public class Trade {
    private Account account;

    public void associateWith(Account account) {
        this.account = account;  // this.account = field. account = parameter.
        account.addTrade(this);  // 'this' = the current Trade object passed to Account
    }
}
```

