CONSTRUCTOR BASICS — rules and features:

```java
public class Account {
    private final String accountId;
    private BigDecimal balance;

    // CONSTRUCTOR: same name as class, NO return type
    public Account(String accountId, BigDecimal initialBalance) {
        this.accountId = accountId;
        this.balance = initialBalance;
        // Called when: new Account("SAV2001", BigDecimal.ZERO)
    }

    // OVERLOADED constructor (multiple constructors):
    public Account(String accountId) {
        this(accountId, BigDecimal.ZERO);  // chains to first constructor
    }

    // NO-ARG constructor (if needed):
    public Account() {
        this("UNKNOWN", BigDecimal.ZERO);
    }
}

// Rules:
// 1. Same name as class
// 2. NO return type (not even void!)
// 3. Called automatically by 'new'
// 4. Can be overloaded (multiple constructors with different params)
// 5. If you write NONE → Java provides default no-arg constructor
// 6. If you write ANY constructor → Java does NOT provide default
```

