SHALLOW COPY — references are shared:

```java
// Account has a nested Address object:
public class Account {
    private String accountId;
    private BigDecimal balance;
    private Address address;  // reference to an Address object

    // SHALLOW COPY: copies references (address SHARED!)
    public Account shallowCopy() {
        Account copy = new Account();
        copy.accountId = this.accountId;     // String is immutable — safe
        copy.balance = this.balance;          // BigDecimal is immutable — safe
        copy.address = this.address;          // REFERENCE copied — SAME object!
        return copy;
    }
}

Account original = new Account("SAV2001", balance, new Address("42 MG Road", "Mumbai"));
Account shallow = original.shallowCopy();

// Both point to the SAME Address object:
shallow.getAddress().setCity("Delhi");
System.out.println(original.getAddress().getCity());  // "Delhi"! ORIGINAL CHANGED!
// Because: shallow.address == original.address → same object in memory!
```

DEEP COPY — everything is independent:

```java
public class Account {
    // DEEP COPY: copies nested objects recursively
    public Account deepCopy() {
        Account copy = new Account();
        copy.accountId = this.accountId;     // immutable — safe either way
        copy.balance = this.balance;          // immutable — safe
        copy.address = new Address(           // NEW Address object — INDEPENDENT!
            this.address.getStreet(),
            this.address.getCity()
        );
        return copy;
    }
}

Account original = new Account("SAV2001", balance, new Address("42 MG Road", "Mumbai"));
Account deep = original.deepCopy();

deep.getAddress().setCity("Delhi");
System.out.println(original.getAddress().getCity());  // "Mumbai"! ORIGINAL UNCHANGED!
// Because: deep.address is a DIFFERENT object than original.address
```

Three ways to deep copy in Java:

```java
// WAY 1: Copy constructor (RECOMMENDED)
public class Account {
    public Account(Account source) {
        this.accountId = source.accountId;
        this.balance = source.balance;
        this.address = new Address(source.address);  // Address also has copy constructor
        this.trades = source.trades.stream()
            .map(Trade::new)  // each trade deep copied via copy constructor
            .collect(Collectors.toCollection(ArrayList::new));
    }
}

// WAY 2: Cloneable + clone() override (DISCOURAGED — broken design)
public class Account implements Cloneable {
    @Override
    public Account clone() {
        try {
            Account copy = (Account) super.clone();  // shallow copy
            copy.address = new Address(this.address);  // manually deep copy nested
            copy.trades = new ArrayList<>(this.trades.stream().map(Trade::clone).toList());
            return copy;
        } catch (CloneNotSupportedException e) { throw new RuntimeException(e); }
    }
}
// Problems: Cloneable is a marker (no method!), CloneNotSupportedException is checked,
// super.clone() is shallow — you must manually deep copy each nested field.

// WAY 3: Serialization (creates deep copy via byte stream)
public static <T extends Serializable> T deepCopy(T object) {
    try {
        ByteArrayOutputStream bos = new ByteArrayOutputStream();
        new ObjectOutputStream(bos).writeObject(object);
        ByteArrayInputStream bis = new ByteArrayInputStream(bos.toByteArray());
        return (T) new ObjectInputStream(bis).readObject();
    } catch (Exception e) { throw new RuntimeException(e); }
}
// Pros: handles ANY depth of nesting automatically
// Cons: slow (serialization overhead), all classes must implement Serializable
```

Why immutable objects are IMMUNE to this problem:

```java
// String, BigDecimal, LocalDate are IMMUTABLE:
// Shallow copy shares the reference BUT the object CANNOT be modified!
// So sharing is SAFE for immutable objects.

Account shallow = original.shallowCopy();
// shallow.accountId and original.accountId point to SAME String "SAV2001"
// But String is immutable — nobody can change "SAV2001" to "HACKED"
// So sharing is fine! No deep copy needed for immutable fields.

// ONLY MUTABLE nested objects need deep copying: Address, List<Trade>, etc.
```

