sections.s2_technical:
BEFORE Java 16 — check + explicit cast:

```java
public void processAccount(Account account) {
    if (account instanceof SavingsAccount) {
        SavingsAccount sa = (SavingsAccount) account;  // EXPLICIT cast — redundant!
        BigDecimal interest = sa.calculateInterest();   // access SavingsAccount-specific method
        sa.deposit(interest);
    } else if (account instanceof CurrentAccount) {
        CurrentAccount ca = (CurrentAccount) account;  // another explicit cast
        ca.checkOverdraft();
    }
    // Three problems:
    // 1. Redundant: we JUST checked instanceof — cast is guaranteed safe, why repeat the type?
    // 2. Error-prone: developer might cast to WRONG type (copy-paste bug)
    // 3. Verbose: 2 lines per type (check + cast)
}
```

AFTER Java 16 — pattern matching eliminates the cast:

```java
public void processAccount(Account account) {
    if (account instanceof SavingsAccount sa) {
        // 'sa' is already cast to SavingsAccount — no explicit cast!
        BigDecimal interest = sa.calculateInterest();
        sa.deposit(interest);
    } else if (account instanceof CurrentAccount ca) {
        ca.checkOverdraft();
    }
    // Cleaner: ONE expression does check + cast + bind
    // Safer: no copy-paste cast error possible
    // Shorter: 1 line instead of 2
}
```

FLOW SCOPING — the variable is only usable where the type is guaranteed:

```java
// ═══ FLOW SCOPING: variable 'sa' available only where type is proven ═══

// In if-block: ✅
if (account instanceof SavingsAccount sa) {
    sa.calculateInterest();  // ✅ sa is SavingsAccount here — guaranteed by instanceof
}
// sa is NOT available here — out of scope!

// With && guard: ✅ (type proven for the right side of &&)
if (account instanceof SavingsAccount sa && sa.getBalance().compareTo(threshold) > 0) {
    // ✅ sa is in scope — instanceof passed, && means sa is SavingsAccount
    sa.calculateInterest();
}

// With || : ❌ (type NOT proven for right side of ||)
// if (account instanceof SavingsAccount sa || sa.getBalance().compareTo(threshold) > 0) {
//     ❌ COMPILE ERROR: sa might not be bound if instanceof fails!
// }

// In negated condition: ✅ in the else branch!
if (!(account instanceof SavingsAccount sa)) {
    // sa NOT available here — instanceof failed
    return;
}
// sa IS available here! Compiler knows: if we reached here, instanceof passed.
sa.calculateInterest();  // ✅ flow scoping!
```

PATTERN MATCHING IN SWITCH (Java 21):

```java
// ═══ switch with pattern matching — most powerful form ═══
public String describeAccount(Account account) {
    return switch (account) {
        case SavingsAccount sa when sa.getBalance().compareTo(new BigDecimal("100000")) > 0
            -> "Premium Savings: " + sa.getAccountId() + " Rs." + sa.getBalance();
        case SavingsAccount sa
            -> "Regular Savings: " + sa.getAccountId();
        case CurrentAccount ca
            -> "Current: " + ca.getAccountId() + " (overdraft: " + ca.getOverdraftLimit() + ")";
        case NRIAccount nri
            -> "NRI: " + nri.getAccountId() + " (" + nri.getCurrency() + ")";
        case null
            -> "No account provided";
        default
            -> "Unknown account type";
    };
    // Each case: type check + cast + bind + optional guard (when clause)
    // Exhaustive: compiler ensures all types are handled
}
```

