sections.s2_technical:
A marker interface has NO abstract methods — it is completely empty:

```java
// ═══ SERIALIZABLE: "this class can be serialized to bytes" ═══
public interface Serializable {
    // ZERO methods! Just a marker.
}

// Usage: JVM checks instanceof Serializable before serializing
public class Account implements Serializable {
    private String accountId;
    private BigDecimal balance;
    // This class can now be written to ObjectOutputStream
}

// Without Serializable:
// ObjectOutputStream.writeObject(account) → throws NotSerializableException!

// With Serializable:
ObjectOutputStream oos = new ObjectOutputStream(new FileOutputStream("account.dat"));
oos.writeObject(account);  // ✅ Works — Account is Serializable
```

```java
// ═══ CLONEABLE: "Object.clone() is allowed" ═══
public interface Cloneable {
    // ZERO methods! Just a marker.
    // clone() is on Object, NOT on Cloneable — confusing design!
}

// Without Cloneable: clone() throws CloneNotSupportedException
public class Trade {
    @Override
    public Trade clone() throws CloneNotSupportedException {
        return (Trade) super.clone();  // throws CloneNotSupportedException!
    }
}

// With Cloneable:
public class Trade implements Cloneable {
    @Override
    public Trade clone() {
        try {
            return (Trade) super.clone();  // ✅ Works — Trade is Cloneable
        } catch (CloneNotSupportedException e) {
            throw new AssertionError("Should never happen — we implement Cloneable");
        }
    }
}
// NOTE: Cloneable is considered a BROKEN design. Prefer copy constructors.
```

```java
// ═══ REMOTE: "this object can be invoked via RMI" ═══
public interface Remote {
    // ZERO methods! Just a marker.
}

public interface TradingService extends Remote {
    TradeResult executeTrade(String symbol, int qty) throws RemoteException;
}
// RMI framework checks instanceof Remote before allowing remote invocation
```

HOW the JVM uses marker interfaces internally:

```java
// Inside ObjectOutputStream (simplified):
public void writeObject(Object obj) throws IOException {
    if (!(obj instanceof Serializable)) {
        throw new NotSerializableException(obj.getClass().getName());
        // Checks the marker at RUNTIME!
    }
    // ... proceed with serialization
}

// Inside Object.clone() (simplified):
protected Object clone() throws CloneNotSupportedException {
    if (!(this instanceof Cloneable)) {
        throw new CloneNotSupportedException();
    }
    // ... perform shallow copy
}
```

MARKER INTERFACE vs ANNOTATION — when to use which:

```java
// MARKER INTERFACE:
// + Can be used as TYPE in generics: <T extends Serializable>
// + Can be checked with instanceof at runtime
// + Defines a TYPE — variable can be declared as: Serializable obj = account;
// - Cannot carry metadata (no attributes)
// - Pollutes the class hierarchy

// ANNOTATION:
// + Can carry metadata: @Cacheable(ttl = 60)
// + Doesn't pollute class hierarchy
// + Can target methods, fields, parameters — not just classes
// + Retained at runtime with @Retention(RUNTIME) for reflection
// - Cannot be used as a TYPE in generics

// MODERN RULE:
// New designs → use annotations (@Entity, @Cacheable, @Transactional)
// Existing JDK → marker interfaces remain (Serializable, Cloneable)
```

Creating a CUSTOM marker interface in banking:

```java
// Custom marker: "this account type supports international transfers"
public interface InternationalTransferCapable {
    // ZERO methods — marker!
}

public class NRIAccount extends Account implements InternationalTransferCapable {
    // NRI accounts can do international transfers
}

public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    // Savings accounts CANNOT — no marker!
}

// Transfer service checks the marker:
public void transferInternational(Account account, BigDecimal amount, String country) {
    if (!(account instanceof InternationalTransferCapable)) {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException(
            account.getClass().getSimpleName() + " does not support international transfers!");
    }
    // proceed with international transfer...
}
```

