sections.s2_technical:
THE PROBLEM — Java's actual `java.util.Stack` extends `Vector`:

```java
// java.util.Stack — the broken design in the JDK itself!
public class Stack<E> extends Vector<E> {
    public E push(E item) { ... }   // Stack-specific: add to top
    public E pop() { ... }           // Stack-specific: remove from top
    public E peek() { ... }          // Stack-specific: look at top
}

// But because it EXTENDS Vector, these are also available:
Stack<String> stack = new Stack<>();
stack.push("A");
stack.push("B");
stack.push("C");

// ❌ BREAKS LIFO CONTRACT:
stack.add(0, "Z");         // insert at BOTTOM! Vector.add(index, element) leaked!
stack.remove(0);           // remove from BOTTOM! Vector.remove(int) leaked!
stack.get(1);              // random access! Vector.get(int) leaked!
stack.set(0, "REPLACED");  // replace at arbitrary position! Leaked!
stack.insertElementAt("X", 1);  // Vector-specific! Leaked!

// The Stack's LIFO contract is DESTROYED — anyone can manipulate it like a list.
// This is WHY the Javadoc says: "A more complete and consistent set of LIFO
// stack operations is provided by the Deque interface and its implementations,
// which should be used in preference to this class."
```

THE FIX — composition hides the internal collection:

```java
// ═══ COMPOSITION-BASED STACK: only push/pop/peek exposed ═══
public class SafeStack<E> {
    private final List<E> elements = new ArrayList<>();  // PRIVATE! Hidden!

    public void push(E item) {
        elements.add(item);  // add to end = top of stack
    }

    public E pop() {
        if (elements.isEmpty()) throw new EmptyStackException();
        return elements.remove(elements.size() - 1);  // remove from end = top
    }

    public E peek() {
        if (elements.isEmpty()) throw new EmptyStackException();
        return elements.get(elements.size() - 1);
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() { return elements.isEmpty(); }
    public int size() { return elements.size(); }

    // NO add(index, item), NO remove(0), NO get(index), NO set(index, item)!
    // LIFO contract ENFORCED — callers can only push/pop/peek.
    // ArrayList is hidden — its methods are NOT part of SafeStack's API.
}
```

Banking example — PriorityQueue broken by inheritance:

```java
// ❌ INHERITANCE: leaks ArrayList methods
public class BrokenPriorityQueue extends ArrayList<Customer> {
    public void enqueue(Customer customer) {
        // Insert in priority order:
        int index = findInsertionPoint(customer.getPriority());
        add(index, customer);
    }

    public Customer dequeueHighest() {
        if (isEmpty()) throw new NoSuchElementException();
        return remove(0);  // highest priority at front
    }
}

// CALLER CAN BYPASS PRIORITY:
BrokenPriorityQueue queue = new BrokenPriorityQueue();
queue.enqueue(new Customer("Rajesh", Priority.HIGH));
queue.enqueue(new Customer("Priya", Priority.LOW));

// ❌ BYPASS! Insert low-priority customer at front:
queue.add(0, new Customer("Hacker", Priority.LOWEST));
// queue.get(0) is now "Hacker" — served first despite LOWEST priority!
// ArrayList.add(index, element) LEAKED through inheritance!

// ✅ COMPOSITION FIX:
public class SafePriorityQueue {
    private final List<Customer> queue = new ArrayList<>();  // PRIVATE!

    public void enqueue(Customer customer) {
        int index = findInsertionPoint(customer.getPriority());
        queue.add(index, customer);  // WE control insertions
    }

    public Customer dequeueHighest() {
        if (queue.isEmpty()) throw new NoSuchElementException();
        return queue.remove(0);
    }

    // NO add(0, customer) exposed! NO get(index)! NO set()!
    // Priority contract ENFORCED.
}
