Anonymous class vs Lambda:

```java
// ═══ BEFORE Java 8: anonymous class for Comparator ═══
Comparator<Trade> oldWay = new Comparator<Trade>() {
    @Override
    public int compare(Trade a, Trade b) {
        return a.getAmount().compareTo(b.getAmount());
    }
};

// ═══ AFTER Java 8: lambda (only for FUNCTIONAL INTERFACE — one abstract method) ═══
Comparator<Trade> newWay = (a, b) -> a.getAmount().compareTo(b.getAmount());
// Shorter! But ONLY works for interfaces with ONE abstract method.

// ═══ ABSTRACT CLASS: lambda DOESN'T work! Must use anonymous class. ═══
// Account lambda = (id, bal) -> { ... };  // ❌ COMPILE ERROR! Account is not a functional interface!
// Lambda works ONLY for interfaces, not abstract classes.
// Must use anonymous subclass for abstract classes.
```

